db/ref 1045 "The Quotient Rule for Exponents With Algebraic Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064459 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065191 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064459 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064459 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064459 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "47f4640b-e4b7-5133-a3a1-63ce3b9d9596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064914 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063540 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064914 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60572" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064914 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064914 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064914 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e67fcd0-74f3-542c-ab05-d6c58bbf219a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064914 #edb/ref 1074 "We subtract the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}} & = \\\\ {a}^{(5-9)} & = \\\\ {a}^{-4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we convert the negative exponent to a positive exponent by taking the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{-4} & =\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{9}}=\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065191 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065191 #edb/ref 1061 3518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065191 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6663dad9-c326-5db6-92b4-d33b07b35ae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065191 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065192 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065192 #edb/ref 1061 3522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065192 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2b04881-6bc8-5920-904c-e6ab5ed0bea7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065192 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065193 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065193 #edb/ref 1061 3524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065193 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5df8da7-4ba5-5203-9fe1-9d3c0405217c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065193 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065194 #edb/ref 1061 3526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065194 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f852560e-1702-515d-ab85-f06ccdd54d76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065194 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1064 "The **quotient rule for exponents** states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ {a}^{(5-2)} & = \\\\ {a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two terms $a^5$ and $a^2$ we have $a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a$ and $a^2 = a \\cdot a.$ If we now substitute these two expressions into $\\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &=\\\n a^3\n}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1065 1478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5ce1b58b-8395-5986-99e6-cfd10bbca653" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065337 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484815 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{{q}^{5}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484815 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484815 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00ddfa3b-7d8d-5460-89cf-64315fc87036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484838 